WEBVTT

00:00:00.058 --> 00:00:02.450
Imagine you have an idea for a small tool.

00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:06.095
You are a performer, and event organizers contact you by

00:00:06.177 --> 00:00:08.150
email, text, and web form.

00:00:08.661 --> 00:00:11.738
You want one place where you can see each inquiry and what

00:00:11.784 --> 00:00:12.690
needs a reply.

00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:16.312
You tell an AI agent, "Build me something that keeps track

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:19.725
of my inquiries." The agent may immediately write code.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.339
That feels like progress.

00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:23.777
But what exactly is an inquiry?

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:25.402
Who uses the tool?

00:00:25.646 --> 00:00:26.947
Should it send messages?

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.372
What happens when two messages describe the same event?

00:00:31.068 --> 00:00:33.715
Until those questions are settled, a fast build can make

00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:35.735
the wrong assumptions very efficiently.

00:00:36.029 --> 00:00:39.175
This course teaches a different starting point: slow down

00:00:39.291 --> 00:00:42.171
long enough to make the important decisions visible.

00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:45.271
Then give the agent a clear path to build and prove the

00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:45.863
result.

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:48.997
We call the journey cloud to contract to code.

00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:51.726
The cloud is the messy idea you start with.

00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:55.325
The contract is the shared description of what must happen

00:00:55.395 --> 00:00:56.939
and how we will know it works.

00:00:57.543 --> 00:00:59.900
The code is the implementation that follows.

00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:02.709
The aim is not to make you a programmer overnight.

00:01:03.034 --> 00:01:05.879
It is to help you lead the conversation and recognize

00:01:05.914 --> 00:01:08.328
whether the resulting work matches what you asked for.

00:01:08.696 --> 00:01:09.741
There are two loops.

00:01:10.066 --> 00:01:13.991
The first is the conversation loop: spill, mirror,

00:01:14.269 --> 00:01:16.812
research, decide, and agree.

00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:20.910
The second is the delivery loop: establish a baseline,

00:01:21.351 --> 00:01:25.566
build, verify, prove, and carry the learning forward.

00:01:26.262 --> 00:01:27.609
A loop can repeat.

00:01:27.992 --> 00:01:30.604
You may discover a contradiction during research and

00:01:30.663 --> 00:01:31.754
return to the readback.

00:01:32.195 --> 00:01:35.063
A failed test can send a change back for correction.

00:01:35.446 --> 00:01:38.174
The order gives us a map, not a reason to ignore new

00:01:38.221 --> 00:01:38.906
information.

00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:42.124
In the conversation loop, you begin with the brain dump.

00:01:42.821 --> 00:01:46.095
Say what you are trying to accomplish, what frustrates

00:01:46.141 --> 00:01:49.473
you, who is involved, what you have tried, and what you

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:50.600
are unsure about.

00:01:51.203 --> 00:01:53.049
You do not need a polished prompt.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.962
You can say, "I have three places where inquiries arrive,

00:01:57.136 --> 00:01:58.634
and I keep losing track.

00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:00.979
I need to see what needs a response.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.800
I am not sure whether this should send emails

00:02:03.847 --> 00:02:06.563
automatically." That uncertainty matters.

00:02:06.888 --> 00:02:09.570
It is part of the idea, not a mistake to hide.

00:02:09.864 --> 00:02:11.211
Next comes the mirror.

00:02:11.536 --> 00:02:15.518
Ask the agent to repeat back the goal, people, desired

00:02:15.553 --> 00:02:19.616
experience, constraints, assumptions, and open questions.

00:02:20.127 --> 00:02:23.447
In our example, it might say, "An organizer submits

00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:27.569
details; the performer sees one inquiry and can mark it as

00:02:27.627 --> 00:02:31.656
needing a reply or handled." You can correct it: "Yes, but

00:02:31.702 --> 00:02:33.862
I want to decide what to send myself.

00:02:34.187 --> 00:02:37.623
Do not send emails automatically." The important thing is

00:02:37.658 --> 00:02:40.979
that the agent describes behavior you can recognize: who

00:02:41.037 --> 00:02:44.044
does what, in what order, and what result they see.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.165
That creates an alignment gate.

00:02:46.548 --> 00:02:48.905
A gate is simply a point where we check whether a

00:02:48.974 --> 00:02:50.971
condition is true before going on.

00:02:51.482 --> 00:02:54.327
Here, the condition is that the owner recognizes the

00:02:54.443 --> 00:02:56.498
readback as the intended experience.

00:02:56.939 --> 00:03:00.630
You can say, "Yes, that is right," or keep correcting it.

00:03:01.072 --> 00:03:04.195
This gate matters because architecture--the plan for how

00:03:04.253 --> 00:03:07.190
the pieces fit together--rests on the behavior we are

00:03:07.213 --> 00:03:08.119
trying to create.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.369
When a task is already agreed and authorized, the agent

00:03:11.404 --> 00:03:14.411
should recognize that and continue from the current stage

00:03:14.457 --> 00:03:16.965
rather than force you to repeat the whole conversation.

00:03:17.286 --> 00:03:20.699
After we understand the intended experience, we research.

00:03:21.024 --> 00:03:23.869
The agent looks at what already exists and separates

00:03:23.915 --> 00:03:26.109
verified facts from plans and assumptions.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.080
Perhaps an inquiry form already stores event details, but

00:03:30.115 --> 00:03:31.670
there is no shared view of them.

00:03:32.054 --> 00:03:35.571
Perhaps a document says automatic email is planned, though

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:37.406
no such feature is actually running.

00:03:37.847 --> 00:03:39.182
Those are different claims.

00:03:39.565 --> 00:03:43.118
This reality scan helps us avoid rebuilding something that

00:03:43.176 --> 00:03:46.241
already works or promising a feature that is only

00:03:46.287 --> 00:03:46.833
imagined.

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:49.338
Then we make the decisions that change the design.

00:03:49.721 --> 00:03:52.542
For the tracker, should the first version accept inquiries

00:03:52.693 --> 00:03:56.583
only from the existing form, or also import old emails?

00:03:57.094 --> 00:03:58.452
What happens to duplicates?

00:03:58.777 --> 00:04:01.540
The agent can describe options, tradeoffs, and a

00:04:01.598 --> 00:04:02.422
recommendation.

00:04:02.864 --> 00:04:05.197
You decide the questions that change the outcome.

00:04:05.522 --> 00:04:09.063
A decision ledger is a simple record of the choice, why it

00:04:09.098 --> 00:04:12.419
was made, and what could justify revisiting it later.

00:04:12.802 --> 00:04:16.030
It keeps a future agent from reopening a settled question

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:18.897
because the original conversation is no longer in its

00:04:18.944 --> 00:04:19.571
context.

00:04:19.927 --> 00:04:22.388
Only then do we describe the architecture and write the

00:04:22.423 --> 00:04:23.526
build specification.

00:04:24.037 --> 00:04:27.380
Architecture maps responsibilities and relationships: a

00:04:27.473 --> 00:04:30.620
form collects information, a record stores it, and a

00:04:30.678 --> 00:04:32.001
private view displays it.

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:35.496
A workflow describes what happens over time, including

00:04:35.565 --> 00:04:36.204
failures.

00:04:36.529 --> 00:04:39.373
A build specification turns that design into precise

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.797
behavior: required inputs, outputs, validation, edge

00:04:43.843 --> 00:04:46.003
cases, and acceptance criteria.

00:04:46.606 --> 00:04:50.148
Acceptance criteria are observable conditions that tell us

00:04:50.183 --> 00:04:51.808
when a requirement has been met.

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.722
"The tracker is easy to use" is hard to prove.

00:04:55.047 --> 00:04:58.751
"A user can find a submitted inquiry and mark it handled"

00:04:58.925 --> 00:05:01.258
gives us a specific behavior to test.

00:05:01.577 --> 00:05:04.364
The delivery loop starts by establishing the baseline:

00:05:04.805 --> 00:05:08.184
what files, version, and running behavior exist now.

00:05:08.625 --> 00:05:10.575
We then authorize the relevant work.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.861
Agreement on a design is not automatically permission to

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:17.947
deploy it, spend money, send messages, or change outside

00:05:17.982 --> 00:05:18.563
systems.

00:05:19.004 --> 00:05:22.034
A kickoff is the concise instruction a new agent can

00:05:22.092 --> 00:05:25.714
follow: where the work lives, which decisions govern it,

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:29.441
what is allowed, which steps come first, and how to verify

00:05:29.488 --> 00:05:30.126
the result.

00:05:30.567 --> 00:05:33.981
If you already authorized a particular action, the agent

00:05:34.016 --> 00:05:36.512
should honor that authorization rather than ask for it

00:05:36.547 --> 00:05:37.000
again.

00:05:37.328 --> 00:05:39.418
Build in small, complete slices.

00:05:39.743 --> 00:05:43.098
For example, first save a fictional inquiry and retrieve

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:43.388
it.

00:05:44.085 --> 00:05:45.432
Next show its status.

00:05:46.244 --> 00:05:49.135
Each slice includes an appropriate check and leaves a

00:05:49.251 --> 00:05:50.435
record of what changed.

00:05:50.877 --> 00:05:53.965
A progress log tracks current work and blockers; a

00:05:54.046 --> 00:05:56.798
changelog records meaningful changes over time.

00:05:57.239 --> 00:06:00.350
A handoff tells the next person or agent what is done,

00:06:00.733 --> 00:06:03.276
what is uncertain, and the next safe step.

00:06:03.973 --> 00:06:06.782
These documents are useful because the next session may

00:06:06.852 --> 00:06:08.733
not have the original conversation.

00:06:08.988 --> 00:06:10.567
Finally, prove the right claim.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.690
"Implemented" means the capability exists in the code.

00:06:14.073 --> 00:06:16.871
"Offline-tested" means a check passed in a controlled

00:06:16.894 --> 00:06:17.475
environment.

00:06:17.858 --> 00:06:20.749
"Provider-accepted" means an outside service accepted a

00:06:20.818 --> 00:06:21.422
request.

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:24.661
"Live-observed" means the real behavior was actually

00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:26.124
observed in the live environment.

00:06:26.565 --> 00:06:28.388
These levels are not interchangeable.

00:06:28.771 --> 00:06:31.952
For example, a test that simulates sending an email does

00:06:31.999 --> 00:06:34.344
not prove that a real organizer received one.

00:06:34.786 --> 00:06:38.141
Evidence is the concrete record behind a claim: a test

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:41.961
result, a sanitized screenshot, a command result, or an

00:06:42.007 --> 00:06:45.165
observed live outcome, with enough context for someone

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:46.094
else to assess it.

00:06:46.422 --> 00:06:48.813
Throughout this course, we will keep returning to the same

00:06:48.848 --> 00:06:49.394
tracker.

00:06:49.835 --> 00:06:52.436
You will not need to write code to practice defining what

00:06:52.494 --> 00:06:55.988
should happen, asking for an architecture, or assessing a

00:06:56.046 --> 00:06:56.523
plan.

00:06:56.906 --> 00:06:59.878
By the end, you will have a set of artifacts that another

00:06:59.936 --> 00:07:01.956
agent can use without guessing what you meant.

00:07:02.397 --> 00:07:05.915
The central habit is straightforward: capture the idea,

00:07:06.194 --> 00:07:09.317
reach agreement on the experience, make the decisions

00:07:09.387 --> 00:07:12.928
visible, and ask for proof at the level you actually need.
